Patched version: upgrade deep-merge-safe to 3.2.2 or later (the prototype-pollution fix was released in 3.2.2). Prefer the latest available 3.x.

If you can’t upgrade immediately, mitigations:
- Block dangerous keys before merging: recursively reject __proto__, constructor, and prototype keys from any untrusted input.
  Example:
  function isSafeKey(k) { return k !== '__proto__' && k !== 'constructor' && k !== 'prototype'; }
  function sanitize(obj, seen = new WeakSet()) {
    if (obj && typeof obj === 'object' && !seen.has(obj)) {
      seen.add(obj);
      for (const k of Object.keys(obj)) {
        if (!isSafeKey(k)) { delete obj[k]; continue; }
        sanitize(obj[k], seen);
      }
    }
    return obj;
  }
  // use sanitize(input) before passing to deep-merge-safe

- Merge into a null-prototype object to avoid prototype poisoning side effects:
  const target = Object.create(null);
  // deep-merge-safe(target, sanitize(source))
  // If needed later: const plain = Object.assign({}, target);

- If you’re on Node 18.19+/20+, run with --disable-proto=throw (or --disable-proto=delete) to neutralize __proto__ as a mutator at runtime.

These are mitigations only; upgrading to >= 3.2.2 is the recommended fix.

