If you can’t upgrade immediately, common, low-risk mitigations for prototype-pollution issues:
1) Strip dangerous keys at trust boundaries and before merging
   - Reject or remove keys named “__proto__”, “prototype”, and “constructor” recursively in request payloads or any untrusted objects.
   - Minimal example:
     function stripProtoKeys(v) {
       if (!v || typeof v !== 'object') return v;
       for (const k of Object.keys(v)) {
         if (k === '__proto__' || k === 'prototype' || k === 'constructor') {
           delete v[k];
           continue;
         }
         stripProtoKeys(v[k]);
       }
       return v;
     }

2) Merge into objects without a prototype when possible
   - Create targets with Object.create(null) and copy only own, enumerable properties. This prevents poisoning Object.prototype via __proto__ assignments.

3) Prefer allowlists/schemas over free-form merge for untrusted input
   - Validate inputs with a schema (e.g., Ajv) and construct the target object explicitly from allowed fields instead of deep-merging arbitrary data.

4) Check for a library flag to ignore prototype keys
   - Some merge/parsing libs expose an option to ignore or disallow prototype-related keys. If “deep-merge-safe” documents such an option, enable it until you can upgrade.

5) Force a patched version once you know it
   - If it’s a transitive dep, use package manager features to pin/override:
     - npm: "overrides"
     - Yarn: "resolutions"
     - pnpm: "overrides"

